ation x0, and no memory controls, so it has no retirement or architectural side effect.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `core_pkg.sv` explicitly ordered first. The requested glob command itself fails because it presents `core.sv` before `core_pkg.sv`, contrary to the package file's stated source-order requirement.\n- Formal passed: 53 checks, 0 failures, using `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`. An initial run that explicitly supplied `checks_si.cfg` selected the default dual-channel wrapper and failed elaboration due the missing channel-1 ports; rerunning without an explicit config let the script auto-select the correct nret=1 wrapper and all checks passed. No RTL fix was needed.\n- Cosim passed: 2 ELF tests with `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`. The default `make cosim TARGET=bench` selects NRET=2 and fails to compile its harness against this core's nret=1 interface; setting NRET=1 aligned the harness to `core.yaml`.\n- Vendor timing flow (`make timing TARGET=bench`): Fmax 46.423 MHz, 21 logic levels, 2650 logic cells, 631 registers. The leading reported path still runs from the register file through ID, EX, IF, hazard, and back into ID; timing confirms that this path remains critical after the change.\n\n## Concerns\n\n- The redirect NOP still decodes to an ADDI control bundle, but `valid=0`, `rd=x0`, and its memory controls are clear. It cannot retire, write a nonzero register, or access memory.\n- The Gowin timing report still identifies the RF-to-EX-to-hazard-to-ID feedback route as critical, so this narrow change did not remove that overall route from the top path list.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"The timing report shows the feedback still passing through the hazard unit because `flush_if` redundantly includes `redirect`, while IF already receives `redirect` directly and emits its invalid NOP from that input. I’m removing redirect from the hazard interface too, so IF owns redirect invalidation and hazard control handles stalls and load-use bubbles. This preserves the redirect behavior while removing that extra hop from the feedback path."}}
