{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git status --short && git diff --check && git diff --stat && sed -n '1,160p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py\n cores/bench/rtl/core.sv           |  5 ++--\n cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv       | 25 ++++++++++------\n cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv       | 63 +++++++++++++++++++++++----------------\n cores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py |  9 ++++--\n 4 files changed, 64 insertions(+), 38 deletions(-)\n# Synchronous replicated register file\n\n## Changes\n\n- Replaced the asynchronous two-read register file with two replicated\n  single-read banks. Each bank has a registered read output, shared writes,\n  synchronous reset clearing, x0 handling, and a same-cycle WB bypass.\n- Kept the existing ID/EX boundary and captured decoded metadata alongside\n  the synchronous RF output registers. The read outputs hold whenever\n  ID/EX holds, keeping operands aligned during load-use, memory, and divider\n  stalls. Existing load-use and forwarding control remains sufficient because\n  execute still sees one-cycle-old ID metadata and operands.\n- Updated the register-file unit test for synchronous reads and WB bypass.\n\n## Deviations and concerns\n\nThe existing ID/EX register serves as the operand-read boundary, so no extra\npipeline stage or hazard class was needed. The reset loop is retained for\narchitectural reset behavior; Gowin mapped the replicated banks to 6 BSRAMs\nand 8 LUT-RAMs in the measured build.\n\nThe worst measured path now starts at the RF read output and goes through EX\nbranch resolution and redirect to the PC. The asynchronous multiport mux is\nremoved, but branch resolution is now the visible timing limit.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint: pass (`make lint TARGET=bench`, which puts `core_pkg.sv`\n  first as required by the compilation-unit typedefs).\n- Formal: pass, 53 checks (`RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash\n  formal/run_all.sh`).\n- Core-local tests: pass, 6 tests (`make test TARGET=bench`).\n- Cosim: pass, selftest and CoreMark (`NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`). The\n  bare `make cosim TARGET=bench` build defaults to `NRET=2`, which does not\n  match this core's declared `nret: 1` port set.\n- Gowin timing, placement option 0: 46.84 MHz, 21 logic levels; worst arrival\n  26.643 ns. Baseline notes reported 46.499 MHz and 22 levels.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"agent_message","text":"Implemented the synchronous replicated register file. The read banks now register operands with same-cycle WB bypass and x0 handling. ID metadata advances in parallel, and the read outputs hold with ID/EX during stalls. The existing hazard and forwarding logic remains sufficient because the ID/EX boundary still feeds execute.\n\nVerification passed: lint, formal (53 checks), all 6 core-local tests, cosim (selftest and CoreMark), and Gowin timing. Timing measured 46.84 MHz with 21 logic levels; the worst path now runs through branch resolution to the PC. Cosim required `NRET=1` to match `core.yaml`.\n\nSee [implementation notes](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-luna_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/implementation_notes.md), [reg_file.sv](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-luna_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv), and [id_stage.sv](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-luna_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv)."}}
