{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_27", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git status --short && git diff -- cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv && sed -n '1,200p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && rg -n 'Formal:|Failed:' formal/last_run-w21YtxzfS.log | tail -5\"", "aggregated_output": " M cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\ndiff --git a/cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv b/cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\nindex 9dd38a4..828c06c 100644\n--- a/cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\n+++ b/cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\n@@ -1,17 +1,16 @@\n // rtl/reg_file.sv\n //\n-// 32 x 32 RV32I integer register file.\n-//   - x0 hardwired to zero (writes silently dropped, reads always 0).\n+// 32 x 32 RV32I integer register file arranged as four banks of eight words.\n+// Each bank performs a local 8-word asynchronous read using the low address\n+// bits; the high address bits select one of the four local results. Writes\n+// are decoded into one bank and one row.\n+//\n+//   - x0 is hardwired to zero (writes silently dropped, reads always 0).\n //   - Two combinational read ports.\n //   - Single synchronous write port.\n-//   - Write-first bypass: a same-cycle write to the read address returns\n-//     the new value. This matches the prior Chisel core's RegFile.scala\n-//     and lets the ID stage see WB-stage writes within the same cycle\n-//     without an extra forwarding mux.\n-//\n-// The reset clears all 32 registers. Distributed-LUT or flop inference is\n-// fine here; explicit BRAM attribution lives on the larger imem/dmem\n-// declarations in soc.sv (phase 5).\n+//   - Write-first bypass: a same-cycle write to a read address returns the\n+//     new value, without adding a pipeline stage.\n+//   - Reset clears all 32 registers.\n //\n // Latency:        write = 1 cycle (synchronous), read = combinational.\n // RVFI fields:    feeds rs1_rdata, rs2_rdata, rd_wdata.\n@@ -29,30 +28,59 @@ module reg_file (\n   input  logic [31:0] w_data\n );\n \n-  logic [31:0] regs [0:31];\