{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Register the execute redirect boundary\n\n## Changes\n\n- Added a pending redirect valid bit and target register in `rtl/core.sv`.\n- EX redirect requests now squash ID and operand work immediately, hold fetch\n  at its current PC for that cycle, and apply the saved target in the following\n  cycle. The IF target application has priority over fetch stalls.\n- Redirect capture waits until EX can advance past an older stalled memory\n  operation, avoiding a duplicate request from a held branch or jump.\n- `hazard_unit` interlocks fetch and invalidates younger work during the\n  capture and pending cycles. Existing static prediction and EX misalignment\n  handling are unchanged.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Verilator lint: passed.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: passed,\n  53 checks and 0 failures (single-channel wrapper selected from `core.yaml`).\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: passed both ELF images, including the\n  stall-driven cosim checks.\n- `make timing TARGET=bench`: placement option 0 completed at 77.563 MHz.\n  The reported worst path was operand PC to EX ALU (18.187 ns, 9 logic cells);\n  the following path crossed EX, ID, and the register file (18.178 ns).\n\n## Concerns\n\nUnpredicted jumps and branch mispredictions incur an extra recovery cycle.\nThe timing sample's worst reported path is still in the EX ALU, so this change\nmay improve the redirect feedback cone without improving overall Fmax.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
