{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Implementation notes\n\n## Change\n\nAdded an explicit `imem_stall` input to `if_stage`, driven by\n`!io_imemReady` in `core.sv`. The fetched instruction is now replaced with a\nNOP only during an instruction-memory stall. A redirect still marks the\nfetch slot invalid and flushes ID/operand state, while leaving `imem_data`\non the instruction payload path.\n\n## Deviations and concerns\n\nThe implementation follows the hypothesis. `flush` and `redirect` continue\nto suppress validity and predicted-taken metadata as before; only the\ninstruction payload mux is now controlled by the distinct fetch-stall\nqualifier. A wrong-path instruction can therefore drive combinational\ndecode and register-file addresses during redirect, but its invalid slot is\nflushed before it can enter the pipeline.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Verilator lint: pass.\n- Formal: pass, 53 checks. The initial invocation omitted the required\n  `RTL_DIR` and `CORE_NAME` environment variables; the corrected invocation\n  auto-selected the nret=1 wrapper and passed.\n- Cosim: pass, 2 ELF tests (selftest and CoreMark), with `NRET=1`. The first\n  invocation used the build script's default `NRET=2` and failed to compile\n  against this nret=1 core; no RTL changes were needed for the corrected run.\n- Gowin timing: place option 0 reported 68.432 MHz. The leading path still\n  runs from `u_operand` through `u_ex`, `u_if`, and `u_id` to the register\n  file, so the measured path remains in the same broad stage chain.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
