an repeatedly mispredict before not-taken resolution invalidates their entries.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r4s0 (regression, -0.97%): Splitting the 32-bit branch comparison into byte predicates still leaves a group-priority network on the forwarding-to-redirect path, so parallel subcomparisons alone do not guarantee a faster redirect.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r4s1 (improvement, +27.24%): Capturing youngest-producer forwarded operands in an operand-stage register separates the forwarding mux from ALU, address, and branch logic; load-use hazards, divider and memory holds, and redirect flushes must span the new boundary to preserve precise operands and retirement.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r5s0 (regression, -2.67%): A hand-built 16-bit carry-select `ALU_ADD` duplicated the high-half adder and added a result mux, whose cost outweighed the shorter carry path in this build; verify mapped timing and area before replacing native addition.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r5s1 (regression, +0.27%): Splitting ADD across the operand/EX boundary puts a forwarded-operand adder before the register while retaining upper-half addition and result selection in EX, so added arithmetic and muxing can outweigh reduced carry depth; check both stage paths after mapping.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r5s2 (regression, -9.51%): Even unconditional direct-JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when IF reconstructs the J-immediate, adds it to PC, aligns the target, and selects it on the fetch-to-PC path; avoiding BTB or direction lookup does not remove the front-end timing cost.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r6s0 (improvement, +4.70%): A dedicated registered load/store address path using a 12-bit low sum plus sign/carry-controlled upper increment or decrement can shorten the dmem address path while leaving general ALU operations intact.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
