{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\")];\nfor x in rows:\n if x.get(\\\"id\\\") in [\\\"hyp-20260930-001-r8s0\\\",\\\"hyp-20260930-001-r9s0\\\",\\\"hyp-20260930-001-r10s0\\\"]:\n  print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"impact\\\",x.get(\\\"delta_pct\\\"),\\\"score\\\",x.get(\\\"fitness\\\"),\\\"ipc\\\",x.get(\\\"ipc_coremark\\\"),\\\"cycles\\\",x.get(\\\"cycles\\\")); print(x.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"motivation\\\",\\\"\\\")); print(x.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\")); print(\\\"changes:\\\",x.get(\\\"changes\\\")); print(\\\"notes\\\",x.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\"))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\")];\nfor x in rows:\n if x.get(\\\"id\\\") in [\\\"hyp-20260930-001-r8s0\\\",\\\"hyp-20260930-001-r9s0\\\",\\\"hyp-20260930-001-r10s0\\\"]:\n  print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"impact\\\",x.get(\\\"delta_pct\\\"),\\\"score\\\",x.get(\\\"fitness\\\"),\\\"ipc\\\",x.get(\\\"ipc_coremark\\\"),\\\"cycles\\\",x.get(\\\"cycles\\\")); print(x.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"motivation\\\",\\\"\\\")); print(x.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\")); print(\\\"changes:\\\",x.get(\\\"changes\\\")); print(\\\"notes\\\",x.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\"))'\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20260930-001-r8s0 Gate dependency stalls by source use impact 1.17 score 147.6 ipc 2e-06 cycles 5051958\nThe hazard unit compares both encoded register fields for every valid instruction. For OP-IMM and load instructions, rs2 bits are immediate bits, so a matching older destination can insert an unnecessary bubble; instructions with no register sources can also stall unnecessarily.\n\nDecode source-use classes from the ID/EX opcode and gate the execute-result and load-use comparisons with them: R-type, store, and branch instructions use both sources; OP-IMM, load, and JALR use rs1 only; other opcodes use neither. This should remove false dependency bubbles in CoreMark without adding a pipeline stage, while preserving waits whenever an instruction actually consumes the producer's value.\n\nchanges: [{'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Connect the ID/EX instruction word to hazard detection for source-use classification.'}, {'file': 'rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'description': 'Gate both execute-result and load-use dependency comparisons by decoded rs1/rs2 usage.'}]\nnotes \n\n hyp-20260930-001-r9s0 Narrow EX branch target adder impact -4.03 score 140.01 ipc 2e-06 cycles 5081214\nThe latest timing path still crosses EX redirect resolution into IF and then\nhazard/decode control. Branch targets currently use a full 32-bit PC-relative\naddition even though a B-immediate is only 13 significant signed bits.\nShortening that target path may improve the measured feedback path without\nadding prediction state or changing instruction latency.\n\nForm the EX branch target by adding the low 13 PC and immediate bits, then\nadjust the upper 19 PC bits by at most one according to the low-sum carry and\nimmediate sign. Keep JAL/JALR target handling unchanged. This is modulo-32-bit\nequivalent to the current addition, while limiting long carry propagation on\nthe branch redirect path, analogous to the narrow effective-address adder\nthat improved timing previously.\n\nchanges: [{'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Replace the full-width branch PC-relative adder with a 13-bit low sum and sign/carry-controlled upper increment or decrement.'}]\nnotes \n\n hyp-20260930-001-r10s0 Share one configurable RV32M multiplier impact -4.95 score 138.67 ipc 2e-06 cycles 5081214\nThe ALU describes separate 64-bit products for signed-by-signed, unsigned-by-unsigned, and signed-by-unsigned multiplication. These variants can create parallel multiplier cones and routing load even though each instruction needs only one product. Reducing duplicated arithmetic is a focused datapath optimization for the FPGA implementation.\n\nReplace the three product expressions with one signed 33-by-33 multiplier whose two leading operand bits are selected from the RV32M operation's signedness. For MUL, the low 32 product bits are identical for every signedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the required signed or unsigned extension and use product bits 63:32. This preserves the architectural results while allowing synthesis to share one multiplier across all four operations, reducing DSP/LUT use and routing pressure that may improve Fmax.\n\nchanges: [{'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': 'Replace the three separately expressed 64-bit multiplication variants with one configurable signed 33-by-33 product while preserving ALTOPS substitutions and each RV32M result slice.'}]\nnotes \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
