{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20260930-001-r7s0\\\") | {title,hypothesis,changes,fmax_mhz,cycles,delta_pct,critical_path}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl; sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md | tail -n 60; ls cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"title\": \"Share branch comparison logic\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The champion's worst path now runs from registered operands through EX,\\nfetch, decode, and the register file. A taken branch makes its operand\\ncomparison part of that redirect feedback path. The current branch logic\\nexpresses signed and unsigned less-than and greater-or-equal tests\\nseparately, which can leave redundant comparator logic in EX.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Express all six RV32I branch conditions using one unsigned less-than\\ncomparison, one equality comparison, and sign-bit correction for signed\\nordering. Derive greater-or-equal conditions as complements of the\\ncorresponding less-than result. This should share comparator work and\\nreduce the branch-resolution delay feeding redirect and fetch, with no\\nchange to pipeline latency, branch semantics, or instruction throughput.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 6,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Share unsigned comparison and equality results across branch conditions, deriving signed ordering from operand sign bits.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Share unsigned comparison and equality results across branch conditions, deriving signed ordering from operand sign bits.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 76.167,\n  \"cycles\": 5448155,\n  \"delta_pct\": 1.37,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_operand/reg_q.rs2_val_0_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_ER_s196\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -8.36,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 18.654,\n    \"logic_cells\": 41,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\",\n      \"cpu/u_if\",\n      \"cpu/u_id\",\n      \"cpu/u_rf\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r1s0 (regression, +3.71%): Explicit one-hot forwarding did not shorten the operand path because MEM/WB priority suppression and per-bit mask-and-OR selection still leave control logic on the wide mux; a case mux may map better.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-\n…[7604 of 10676 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ead9a445ef2af3c6]…\n indexed valid/counter selection adds delay to the fetch-to-PC path, so learning forward-branch bias must save enough redirects to repay that lookup cost.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r13s0 (regression, -3.13%): Carrying IF's PC+4 through ID/EX regressed despite removing EX increments, suggesting the 32-bit pipeline payload can cost more in state and routing than recomputing a constant increment when that adder is not proven critical.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r13s1 (regression, -6.62%): The DIV_CORRECT state adds a guaranteed completion cycle while EX stays held, so registering divider sign correction needs enough measured timing gain to repay even this divider-only CoreMark latency.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r13s2 (regression, -0.45%): Two global 2-bit counters by displacement sign regress slightly because unrelated forward branches share and can cancel one learned bias, while IF still adds direction selection; class-wide history must save enough redirects to beat static backward-taken prediction.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
