{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,260p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r1s0 (regression, +3.71%): Explicit one-hot forwarding did not shorten the operand path because MEM/WB priority suppression and per-bit mask-and-OR selection still leave control logic on the wide mux; a case mux may map better.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r1s1 (improvement, +754.13%): When combinational DIV/REM dominates the execute path, a shared iterative divider can remove that cone from the single-cycle ALU and sharply improve timing, provided the EX hold and older-stage drain handshake preserves stalls and retires the divide once.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.65%): IF-stage branch decode, target addition, and the predicted-PC mux put prediction on the fetch-to-PC path, where added front-end delay can outweigh the EX redirects saved on loop back-edges.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r2s0 (regression, +3.02%): Removing redirect from the wide ID/EX clear condition did not shorten the register-file→EX-redirect→IF/hazard→ID feedback path, so optimize the full redirect feedback cone rather than assuming a narrower bundle mux moves timing.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 (regression, -5.33%): Removing IF target addition did not make prediction timing-free: current-instruction branch qualification plus BTB tag/counter lookup and next-PC selection still burden the fetch-to-PC path enough to outweigh saved EX redirects.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r3s1 (regression, -1.49%): Registering EX redirects adds resolve-interlock bubbles for every branch or jump and another front-end flush when a redirect is pending, so CoreMark control-transfer throughput can outweigh the shorter feedback path.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r3s2 (regression, -19.18%): A target-only BTB treats every exact-PC hit as taken, so mixed-outcome conditional branches lose direction history and can repeatedly mispredict before not-taken resolution invalidates their entries.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r4s0 (regression, -0.97%): Splitting the 32-bit branch comparison into byte predicates still \n…[6125 of 9197 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a9dd67cddd64c1c7]…\nting the 32-bit pipeline payload can cost more in state and routing than recomputing a constant increment when that adder is not proven critical.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r13s1 (regression, -6.62%): The DIV_CORRECT state adds a guaranteed completion cycle while EX stays held, so registering divider sign correction needs enough measured timing gain to repay even this divider-only CoreMark latency.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r13s2 (regression, -0.45%): Two global 2-bit counters by displacement sign regress slightly because unrelated forward branches share and can cancel one learned bias, while IF still adds direction selection; class-wide history must save enough redirects to beat static backward-taken prediction.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r14s0 (regression, -19.00%): Routing branch conditions through the ALU's 33-bit subtract flags keeps zero reduction, borrow extraction, and signed-overflow correction in the redirect cone, so a shared carry-chain datapath can map slower than separate comparisons.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
