{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"The recent timing paths still run through EX→IF→ID→the register file. One promising micro optimization is to let the redirect-flushed instruction reach ID’s combinational decode while keeping ID’s register clear and its valid bit low; that may remove redirect from the wide fetch-to-register-file cone. I’m checking that this interaction hasn’t already been tried."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'\nfor line in open(p):\n    x=json.loads(line)\n    if x.get('category')=='micro_opt':\n        title=x.get('title','')\n        lesson=x.get('lesson','')\n        mot=x.get('motivation',{})\n        if isinstance(mot,dict): mot=mot.get('motivation','')\n        hyp=x.get('hypothesis',{})\n        if isinstance(hyp,dict): hyp=hyp.get('hypothesis','')\n        print(x.get('id'), x.get('outcome'), x.get('delta_pct'), title)\n        if any(t in (title+' '+str(mot)+' '+str(hyp)+' '+str(lesson)).lower() for t in ('redirect', 'flush', 'register file', 'regfile')):\n            print('  ',str(lesson or hyp).replace('\\\\n',' ')[:480])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'\nfor line in open(p):\n    x=json.loads(line)\n    if x.get('category')=='micro_opt':\n        title=x.get('title','')\n        lesson=x.get('lesson','')\n        mot=x.get('motivation',{})\n        if isinstance(mot,dict): mot=mot.get('motivation','')\n        hyp=x.get('hypothesis',{})\n        if isinstance(hyp,dict): hyp=hyp.get('hypothesis','')\n        print(x.get('id'), x.get('outcome'), x.get('delta_pct'), title)\n        if any(t in (title+' '+str(mot)+' '+str(hyp)+' '+str(lesson)).lower() for t in ('redirect', 'flush', 'register file', 'regfile')):\n            print('  ',str(lesson or hyp).replace('\\\\n',' ')[:480])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-928db21 improvement 0.0 Baseline retest for cores/bench\nhyp-20260929-001-r1s0 regression 3.71 One-hot EX forwarding mux\nhyp-20260929-001-r2s0 regression 3.02 Remove redundant redirect flush in ID\n   - 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r2s0 (regression, +3.02%): Removing redirect from the wide ID/EX clear condition did not shorten the register-file→EX-redirect→IF/hazard→ID feedback path, so optimize the full redirect feedback cone rather than assuming a narrower bundle mux moves timing.\nhyp-20260929-001-r3s0 regression -0.44 Share branch comparison logic\n   In EX, compute equality once and an unsigned less-than once, derive signed less-than from the operand sign bits and the unsigned result, then select BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU from those shared predicates. Complement the shared predicate for BNE, BGE, and BGEU. This preserves branch semantics while avoiding duplicate 32-bit comparison cones in the critical redirect path. \nhyp-20260929-001-r4s0 regression -0.97 Segmented branch comparator for faster redirects\n   - 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r4s0 (regression, -0.97%): Splitting the 32-bit branch comparison into byte predicates still leaves a group-priority network on the forwarding-to-redirect path, so parallel subcomparisons alone do not guarantee a faster redirect.\nhyp-20260929-001-r5s0 regression -2.67 Carry-select ALU addition\nhyp-20260929-001-r6s0 improvement 4.7 Narrow effective-address adder for memory ops\nhyp-20260930-001-r7s0 regression 1.37 Share branch comparison logic\n   - 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r7s0 (regression, +1.37%): Sharing signed branch ordering through a sign-bit correction mux can leave that mux after the unsigned comparator on the EX redirect path, so fewer comparison operators in RTL do not guarantee a shorter mapped path.\nhyp-20260930-001-r8s0 regression 1.17 Gate dependency stalls by source use\nhyp-20260930-001-r9s0 regression -4.03 Narrow EX branch target adder\n   Form the EX branch target by adding the low 13 PC and immediate bits, then adjust the upper 19 PC bits by at most one according to the low-sum carry and immediate sign. Keep JAL/JALR target handling unchanged. This is modulo-32-bit equivalent to the current addition, while limiting long carry propagation on the branch redirect path, analogous to the narrow effective-address adder that improved timing previously. \nhyp-20260930-001-r10s0 regression -4.95 Share one configurable RV32M multiplier\nhyp-20260930-001-r11s0 regression -7.54 Bank the register-file read mux\n   - 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r11s0 (regression, -7.54%): Explicit four-bank async reads left the register-file feedback path critical in the sampled placement (71.397 MHz versus 74.129 MHz recorded), showing that RTL banking alone may not make FPGA mux mapping more local.\nhyp-20260930-001-r12s0 regression 4.39 Forward load data into the operand stage\n   - 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r12s0 (regression, +4.39%): A ready MEM-load bypass can remove load-use bubbles, but routing dmem_rdata through load extraction and forwarding selection into operand capture adds a long input path whose timing cost can outweigh CoreMark's cycle savings.\nhyp-20260930-001-r13s0 regression -3.13 Reuse fetched PC plus four in execute\n   - 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r13s0 (regression, -3.13%): Carrying IF's PC+4 through ID/EX regressed despite removing EX increments, suggesting the 32-bit pipeline payload can cost more in state and routing than recomputing a constant increment when that adder is not proven critical.\nhyp-20260930-001-r14s0 regression -19.0 Use subtract flags for branch comparisons\n   - 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r14s0 (regression, -19.00%): Routing branch conditions through the ALU's 33-bit subtract flags keeps zero reduction, borrow extraction, and signed-overflow correction in the redirect cone, so a shared carry-chain datapath can map slower than separate comparisons.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
