{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r8s0.yaml')\np.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20260930-001-r8s0\ntitle: Gate dependency stalls by source use\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The hazard unit compares both encoded register fields for every valid instruction. For OP-IMM and load instructions, rs2 bits are immediate bits, so a matching older destination can insert an unnecessary bubble; instructions with no register sources can also stall unnecessarily.\nhypothesis: |\n  Decode source-use classes from the ID/EX opcode and gate the execute-result and load-use comparisons with them: R-type, store, and branch instructions use both sources; OP-IMM, load, and JALR use rs1 only; other opcodes use neither. This should remove false dependency bubbles in CoreMark without adding a pipeline stage, while preserving waits whenever an instruction actually consumes the producer's value.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect the ID/EX instruction word to hazard detection for source-use classification.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Gate both execute-result and load-use dependency comparisons by decoded rs1/rs2 usage.\n''')\nprint(p)\nPY\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport yaml, json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r8s0.yaml')\ndata=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nschema=json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text())\ntry:\n import jsonschema\n jsonschema.validate(data, schema)\n print('schema validation passed')\nexcept ImportError:\n print('PyYAML loaded; jsonschema package unavailable')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
