{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|category: structural|structural' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -60\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260929-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Iterative shared divider in execute\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline worst path runs from the forwarding network through EX and the ALU to the data-memory outputs, with 156 logic cells and 199.837 ns arrival time. The combinational quotient and remainder operators can create a deep variable divide cone in this path and duplicate large arithmetic hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline worst path runs from the forwarding network through EX and the ALU to the data-memory outputs, with 156 logic cells and 199.837 ns arrival time. The combinational quotient and remainder operators can create a deep variable divide cone in this path and duplicate large arithmetic hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU from the combinational ALU into one radix-2 iterative execute unit that shares quotient/remainder work and runs only for M-extension divide instructions. Add a busy/done handshake that holds the divide instruction and prevents younger instructions from advancing until its result is ready, while allowing older instructions to drain and retire once. Keep multiply and ordinary ALU operations single-cycle, preserve RV32M corner cases and ALTOPS behavior, and respect existing instruction/data bus stalls. Removing the divide cone from the normal ALU result path should shorten the critical path and reduce logic; CoreMark should retain nearly the same cycles if its hot loop does not execute division.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 10, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove combinational DIV/REM operators from the no