{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20260929-002-r3s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20260930-002-r8s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20260930-003-r7s2\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\" + .id + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + .title + \\\"\\\\nMOT: \\\" + ((.hypothesis.motivation // \\\"\\\")|gsub(\\\"\\\\n\\\";\\\" \\\")) + \\\"\\\\nHYP: \\\" + ((.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\"\\\")|gsub(\\\"\\\\n\\\";\\\" \\\")) + \\\"\\\\nNOTES: \\\" + (.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\nCYCLES \\\" + (.cycles|tostring) + \\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20260929-002-r3s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20260930-002-r8s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20260930-003-r7s2\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\" + .id + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + .title + \\\"\\\\nMOT: \\\" + ((.hypothesis.motivation // \\\"\\\")|gsub(\\\"\\\\n\\\";\\\" \\\")) + \\\"\\\\nHYP: \\\" + ((.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\"\\\")|gsub(\\\"\\\\n\\\";\\\" \\\")) + \\\"\\\\nNOTES: \\\" + (.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\nCYCLES \\\" + (.cycles|tostring) + \\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"ID: hyp-20260929-002-r3s1\nRegister EX redirects at the pipeline boundary\nMOT: The measured worst path still runs from the register file through EX branch resolution, fetch, and hazard control back to ID, with about 28 ns arrival time. The redirect target and decision currently feed the PC and pipeline controls in the same cycle as operand forwarding and branch comparison. \nHYP: Carry the resolved branch or jump redirect through the existing EX/MEM register and apply it to the fetch PC in the following cycle. In the resolution cycle, interlock fetch and clear the younger ID/EX work; in the redirect cycle, squash the front end before any wrong-path instruction can reach memory or start a multi-cycle operation. This adds a control-transfer bubble but separates the EX compare/target path from the PC and hazard feedback path, which should raise Fmax enough to improve CoreMark fitness overall. \nNOTES: \nCYCLES 5084609\n\nID: hyp-20260930-003-r7s2\nPredict aligned backward branches with a narrow target path\nMOT: CoreMark loop back-edges currently redirect from EX and flush younger instructions. Prior branch predictors recovered some of those cycles, but their fetch-time table lookup or full-width target logic reduced Fmax. \nHYP: Add a static backward-taken, forward-not-taken predictor for valid, aligned conditional branches. Compute the B-type target using a 13-bit low-part sum and sign/carry-controlled upper increment or decrement, avoiding a full 32-bit fetch-path adder. Carry the predicted-taken bit with the instruction and make EX redirect on an outcome mismatch, while retaining the existing misaligned-target trap behavior. The smaller target cone may preserve the loop-recovery IPC gain without the timing penalty seen in earlier predictors. \nNOTES: \nCYCLES 5081214\n\nID: hyp-20260930-002-r8s1\nDecouple redirect validity from fetched instruction\nMOT: The champion's measured worst path runs from the registered operand through EX and IF into ID and the register file. A late branch redirect currently selects a NOP on the IF instruction bus, so redirect control propagates through decode and register-file read selection even though ID is flushed on that same redirect. \nHYP: Separate the fetched instruction payload from redirect validity. Keep the existing NOP substitution for instruction-memory stalls, but let a redirect leave the fetched payload untouched while marking it invalid and relying on the existing ID and operand flushes to discard it. This removes the redirect-controlled instruction mux from the EX-to-ID/register-file path while preserving wrong-path suppression and bus-stall behavior, potentially improving Fmax without adding branch bubbles. \nNOTES: \nCYCLES 5081214\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
