{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -i 'predict|BTB|branch|bimodal|gshare|RAS' cores/bench/LESSONS.md cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260929-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict backward branches at fetch\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Taken loop back-edges currently redirect from EX, flushing the younger\\ninstructions fetched along the sequential path. CoreMark executes many\\nloop branches, so avoiding those flushes can improve iterations per cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Taken loop back-edges currently redirect from EX, flushing the younger\\ninstructions fetched along the sequential path. CoreMark executes many\\nloop branches, so avoiding those flushes can improve iterations per cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a static backward-taken, forward-not-taken predictor in IF for valid,\\naligned conditional branches. Carry its predicted-taken bit through ID/EX\\nand make EX redirect only when the resolved branch disagrees. This should\\nremove the two-cycle recovery cost on taken loop back-edges while retaining\\nthe existing recovery behavior for forward branches and mispredictions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the prediction bit to the IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Predict aligned backward conditional branches taken and redirect fetch to their target.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the fetch prediction bit into the EX-stage pipeline register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare branch resolution with the carried prediction and recover only on a miss.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the prediction bit to the IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Predict aligned backward conditional branches taken and redirect fetch to their target.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the fetch prediction bit into the EX-stage pipeline register.\"}, {\"file\": \n…[53577 of 56649 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:72546f4a1d3b05c3]…\n the new boundary to preserve precise operands and retirement.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:12:- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r5s2 (regression, -9.51%): Even unconditional direct-JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when IF reconstructs the J-immediate, adds it to PC, aligns the target, and selects it on the fetch-to-PC path; avoiding BTB or direction lookup does not remove the front-end timing cost.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:14:- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r6s1 (regression, -0.14%): A ready execute-to-operand bypass removes dependent-ALU bubbles but puts the producer's ALU result and forwarding priority mux on the operand-register input path, where timing cost can erase the cycle savings.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:15:- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r6s2 (regression, -12.91%): Registering BTB metadata alongside the selected PC moves the lookup off the PC feedback mux but leaves a fetch-cycle tag/target path, while wrong predictions still need EX recovery, so a lookup register alone may not repay predictor cost in CoreMark.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; p=\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(p)]; [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"title\\\"],r.get(\\\"delta_pct\\\"),\\\"\\\\n\\\",r.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\"))) for r in rows if r.get(\\\"category\\\")==\\\"predictor\\\"]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; p=\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(p)]; [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"title\\\"],r.get(\\\"delta_pct\\\"),\\\"\\\\n\\\",r.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\"))) for r in rows if r.get(\\\"category\\\")==\\\"predictor\\\"]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n hyp-20260929-003-r1s2 Predict backward branches at fetch -1.65 \n Add a static backward-taken, forward-not-taken predictor in IF for valid,\naligned conditional branches. Carry its predicted-taken bit through ID/EX\nand make EX redirect only when the resolved branch disagrees. This should\nremove the two-cycle recovery cost on taken loop back-edges while retaining\nthe existing recovery behavior for forward branches and mispredictions.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 Tagged branch target buffer -5.33 \n Add an 8-entry direct-mapped BTB indexed by fetch PC, with exact PC tags,\ntarget addresses, and 2-bit saturating direction counters. Qualify lookups\nwith the current instruction's valid branch/jump encoding; update entries\nfrom resolved branches and jumps in EX. Carry the predicted next PC to EX\nand redirect only when the resolved next PC differs. This should remove\nrepeated taken-loop recovery while keeping fetch prediction to a table lookup\nand next-PC mux instead of an immediate adder.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-003-r3s2 Minimal taken-target branch cache -19.18 \n Replace the branch-qualified, direction-counter BTB with a small direct-mapped cache of exact PC tags and resolved taken targets. Predict a target on a tag hit without decoding the current instruction or consulting a direction counter; install targets for resolved taken branches and jumps, and invalidate a matching branch entry when it resolves not taken. Recover in EX whenever the actual next PC differs from the carried prediction. This removes fetch-time branch qualification and counter selection while still avoiding EX recovery on recurrent taken loop edges and calls.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-003-r4s2 Small return-address stack predictor -5.78 \n Add a shallow return-address stack that predicts only canonical JALR returns\nthrough x1 or x5 (rd=x0, immediate=0). Fetch uses the stack top as the target\nonly when the current instruction matches that return form and fetch can\nadvance. Push pc+4 for resolved calls that write x1 or x5, and pop when a\nreturn resolves; carry the predicted target with the instruction and let EX\nredirect on a target mismatch. This avoids a general BTB lookup, branch\ndirection table, or fetch-time immediate adder, while eliminating recovery\nfor correctly predicted function returns common in CoreMark.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-003-r5s2 Predict direct JAL targets in fetch -9.51 \n Recognize direct JAL instructions in IF and form their target from the\nencoded J-immediate, advancing fetch to an aligned target while carrying a\npredicted-next-PC marker with the instruction. Leave misaligned targets to\nthe existing EX trap path. In EX, compare the resolved JAL next PC against\nthe prediction and issue recovery only on a mismatch. This should remove\nthe EX redirect penalty for repeated direct calls and tail calls with less\npredictor logic than a general BTB, without changing conditional branches.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-003-r6s2 Register-ahead branch target prediction -12.91 \n Add a small tagged target table with direction state, but pipeline its lookup\none fetch step ahead: whenever the PC advances, capture the BTB prediction for\nthe selected next PC alongside that PC. The current PC then uses registered\nprediction metadata to select a target, keeping tag comparison and table\nselection out of the same-cycle PC feedback mux. Train entries from resolved\nbranches and jumps, and preserve the existing EX recovery on a prediction\nmiss. This should recover cycles on repeated taken loop branches without the\nfetch-path delay of a combinational BTB lookup.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
