{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"mkdir -p cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses && cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-003-r8s2.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20260930-003-r8s2\ntitle: \\\"Predict nearby direct JAL calls\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current static predictor recovers cycles on aligned backward branches, but direct JAL calls still wait for EX to redirect fetch. The earlier all-range JAL predictor regressed after adding a full-width target calculation to the fetch path. Most local calls can be covered with a short signed displacement and a narrow target path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Extend the existing 13-bit fetch target adder to predict direct JAL instructions whose signed displacement fits in 13 bits, and whose computed target is instruction-aligned. Reuse the branch predictor's low-part sum and carry-controlled upper adjustment rather than calculating a full 32-bit J target in IF. Carry the existing predicted-taken bit through the pipeline and suppress the EX redirect for a predicted JAL; retain EX recovery for long-range or misaligned JALs and all JALRs. This should remove redirect bubbles for common nearby calls while limiting the Fmax cost that hurt the previous direct-JAL experiment.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Recognize JALs with a signed 13-bit displacement, share the narrow target calculation with backward branches, and predict only aligned targets.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Use the existing prediction metadata to suppress redirects for predicted direct JALs while preserving recovery for unpredicted jumps and misalignment traps.\\\"\nEOF\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
